cartridge "CELL RUNNER/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1986"

' CELL RUNNER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Cell Runner" by John Krutch, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette disk of September 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000,
' started by a four-line BASIC boot; the author's name is in the program:
' "CELL RUNNER JOHN KRUTCH 1986"). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's, read from its
' machine code (orig/dis.txt) and watched in a C64 emulator; the game is
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision with hexagonal tile cells, a smooth runner
' sprite, a title tune and sound.
'
' The game: a field of 8 x 10 hexagonal cells lies between a start bar at
' the bottom and a goal bar at the top. Run up from the start bar to the
' goal bar, moving only onto cells that are there. About every second the
' field changes: a new random pattern of cells is shown, and if the cell
' you stand on is gone you fall down to the next cell below (or to the
' start bar). 30 seconds a level; reach the goal and the seconds left plus
' 10 are added to the score. 40 levels, each with a quicker change. Hold
' the stick up for 20 tries in a row and you are sent back to the start
' (let go now and then).
'
' From the original (orig/dis.txt): the pattern table at $C759 (168
' bytes, 1 = no cell); a field is the 80 bytes from a random offset 0-87,
' never the same offset twice in a row ($C43D); the field is redrawn by a
' CIA 2 timer interrupt every 611 x (1536 - 32 x (level - 1)) cycles
' ($C520, about 57 frames at level 1); a move is refused into a missing
' cell; standing on a missing cell drops you one cell at a time
' ($C465); the up count ($C821) goes up by one per pass with the stick
' straight up, back to 0 on any other reading, and at 20 puts you on the
' start bar ($C422); the time is 30 - the CIA clock's seconds ($C575), out
' of time at 0; a level adds the seconds left + 10 in BCD ($C5C9); the
' cell colour goes yellow, grey, cyan, white, green and round again
' ($C4B0); after level 40 the game ends ("YOU'RE OUT-- GOOD WORK.");
' start place: the start bar under the fourth column; diagonals move
' sideways only; a warning blip at each change in the last 10 seconds.
'
' Liberties: one move every 5 frames while the stick is held, the runner
' gliding from cell to cell (the C64 moved a cell about every 1.5 frames,
' too quick to steer); so the up count is one per move tried, 20 tries
' (blocked tries count, as on the C64); the field changes every
' 120 - 2 x (level - 1) frames, about twice the C64's period, from 120 to
' 42, to match the slower moves; cells that will vanish at the next change
' crumble 18 frames before (the C64 gave no warning); a changed field is
' drawn in two halves on two frames (the lower half one frame late); a fall glides 3
' frames a cell; a new field is never the old one, as the C64; best score
' kept; sounds for steps, falls and changes, a title tune and a credits
' page; "OUT OF TIME" / "GOOD WORK" then the title (the C64 said PRESS
' THE FIRE BUTTON TO PLAY AGAIN).
'
' Controls: joystick (either controller): up, down, left, right move one
' cell; on the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

